# Phase 18 Java factory scheduler injection compatibility

- Recorded: 2026-10-23 11:40:31 UTC
- Workspace: `/Users/bruce.tsai/Projects/trio/rocketmq-classic`
- Starting revision: `e0937f80`
- Pinned Java source: RocketMQ 5.5.1, `efa1be59d6fc747ad220f036c86030f05f0419f9`
- Scope: `MQClientAPIFactory` constructor scheduler parameter and current Rustdoc export reconciliation

## Finding and change

The pinned Java class exposes two public constructors, both accepting a
`ScheduledExecutorService`; the seven-argument overload additionally accepts
`ObjectCreator<RemotingClient>`. Rust already mapped remoting-client creation
but both constructor paths always used factory-owned Tokio scheduling. This
left Java's scheduler-injection capability unavailable to Rust callers.

Core now exposes `FactoryRefreshScheduler`, `FactoryRefreshTask`, and
`FactoryRefreshFuture`, plus `MQClientApiFactory::with_refresh_scheduler`.
Domain discovery registers one callback per client at a 10-second initial
delay and 120-second fixed rate. The caller owns the injected scheduler. The
default constructors retain Tokio-owned workers. Registration rejection maps
to `MQClientApiFactoryError::RefreshScheduleFailed` after already-started
clients remain visible, preserving startup order.

The same audit refreshed the distinct public Rustdoc name set. Counts are
Common 439, Consumer 460, Core 744, Producer 362, Protocol 1,524, and Remoting
337. Compared with the 2026-10-22 `1dc3a7e2` baseline, Core adds 62 names and
Consumer adds 15, with no removed names. The four Core names since the prior
scan are the scheduler trait, its two callback aliases, and the builder.

